/bin/td from the Micronix 1.6 distribution

There is no source for td, so this is the disassembly, and td.c is that
disassembly read back into C.

    disas -f td.ctl td.dist

td.dist is the binary off inode 21 of disks/dist/1012-8_dist_2.IMD, 21326
bytes, mode 4711, owner root, dated 7 August 1982.  It is identical to the one
that has always been in filesystem/bin.  It is a Whitesmith a.out - 99 94, the
text at 0100 for 46cb bytes and the data at 5000 - built with the older of the
two runtimes, the one login, init and entab have: c.ent at 4770 and c.ent2 at
477b, saving the register variable cells at 5c6d, 5c6f and 5c71.

THE FILE THAT STOOD HERE BEFORE this one was not a faithful reconstruction,
whatever it was read from, and got the thing the program is for wrong: it made a block device a flat stream of "pathname,
newline, size, bytes" records, a format that is nowhere in the binary.  What
td writes is file systems.  The manual page says so - "this program's back-ups
are in file system form, meaning it is easy to look through a dump", and
restoring is "mount /dev/dja /f; cd /f; cp file ..." - and the disassembly
agrees: a device is mounted on a directory made for it in /tmp, the tree is
copied into that, and when the disk fills, the next is mounted.  That earlier
version also had none of the options, no dump table that was in the form the
binary has, no media change, and no interaction.  Nothing of it is kept.

THE FUNCTIONS, in address order.  Td's own code is 010b to 1a73 and the library
is after it; the names are what each does.

    012d  main        setup; the walk; "Tree dump complete."; cleanup(1)
    0164  options     one argument that begins with a dash, a letter at a time
    03b1  setup       the command line, and everything before the walk
    06ef  interact    the questions asked when nothing is said
    07db  dump        the walk, begun
    0847  copyfile    one file, and the next disk if it fills
    0ace  visit       one thing found in the tree
    0cbd  errs        write(2, s, strlen(s))
    0ce2  errn        a number to the same
    0d19  exists
    0d46  dirname     into a static buffer
    0e95  basename
    0ed1  walk        the tree, a directory at a time
    1058  sigoff      signal(i, 1) for 1 to 15 but 7, remembering the old
    10a6  catch       the handler: cleanup(0)
    10b4  sigon       catch the ones that were not ignored
    1108  isatty
    1133  ask         fputs the prompt, gets the answer; end of file is the end
    12a6  newvolume   unmount, ask for the next disk, mount it
    1447  cleanup     the end, by any way
    155e  initsig
    1587  wanted      whether this file is dumped
    16ca  mkpath      mkdir -p, by mknod and two links - newuser's, and the library's
    188e  dtfind      /etc/dtab: the time of the last dump
    1977  dtput       /etc/dtab: write it
    1a75  isfs        whether a device holds a file system

WHAT IT DOES

td [-v] [-a] [-i] [-u] [-hN] [-dN] [source [destination]].  The first word
that does not begin with a dash is the source and the second the destination.
It will not run unless the standard input, output and error are all terminals -
gtty of each - and says so, on the standard output, if they are not.  The
reason is the media change.

The options are read a letter at a time, so "-vai" is three.  -v is verbose and
-a lifts the limit of a thousand blocks on the size of a file, -i is an
incremental dump and -u updates /etc/dtab at the end.  -h and -d take a number,
the number of hours and of days: the time to go back to starts as the time the
command began, and each -h or -d takes N times 3600 or 86400 seconds off it, so
they add, as the manual says: -d2 -h12 is two and a half days.  A letter that is
none of these is not an error: it is the interactive mode, below.

With no source, or with a source and no destination (that is a repair: the
original stat'ed a null pointer), it asks.  It prints "Td is used to dump an
entire tree of files and directories.", asks for "Source directory: " and
"Destination device: ", and ends if either is empty; and then, until it gets
an F or an I in either case, the three lines "You can dump all the files in
the directory or / only those files that have changed since / the last
incremental dump of this directory" and "Do you want a full or an incremental
dump (F/I) ? ".  An I is -i and -u.  It also turns verbose on.

The source is stat'ed, and for -i the device and inode of it are looked up in
/etc/dtab.  That file is a string of eight byte records - the device, the inode
and the time, a word, a word and a long - and a source that has none is
reported, "Previous table entry not found - defaulting to full dump", and
dumped in full.  A record found is the time to go back to, and it replaces
anything -h and -d made, which is why they are for a dump that is not
incremental.  -u writes the record, over the old one if there is one and at the
end if there is not, when the dump has finished - and only then: the exit
that the original called with a 1 for a good dump is the one that does it.

The destination is a block special file or a directory.  A directory is where
the files go.  A device is mounted on a directory the program makes with mknod,
"/tmp/<pid>-<clock mod 10000>", and the first disk goes through the same
routine as every later one: the one mounted is unmounted, "Insert new media and
press RETURN to continue / or type the name of a new device containing a file
system." is said and "\t-> " is the prompt, and a name typed is the device from
then on.  Whether a device holds a file system is whether the block that is the
first of the data - two past the inode blocks, which is read out of the
superblock - begins with "." and ".." that are both inode 1.  If it does not,
"<device>: Not a file system" is said and "Do you want to make a file system on
this device?" asked, and a Y runs "mkfs <device>" with sh -c, and is asked again.
If it does, the device is unmounted first - a busy one is "The device is mounted
and someone else is using it just now!" - and then mounted.  From the second
disk on it says "The previous file system should be labeled volume #n".

The walk is the one find has: a directory, its entries, and into each.  Every
path is built in one buffer by appending.  The root of it, and "." and "..",
are visited and passed over, and so is the destination directory if it is in the
tree.  What is done to each:

    a regular file   if it is wanted, copied; mode & 06777, the owner, parents made
    a directory      made, with mknod, mode 0777, and given to nobody
    anything else    made again, mknod with the mode and the number, if the
                     super-user is dumping; named and passed over if not

A file is wanted if it was changed at or after the time to go back to (the
whole of time unless there is one) and is not over a thousand blocks, or -a was
said.  If it is not, " - Not dumped." is written, with the name in front of it
if -v - the name is conditional and the rest of the line is not, so a quiet dump
writes the bare " - Not dumped." for each one.  That is the original's, and
td.c's.  If it is, and -v, "name - n Blocks".

The copy is in eight thousand byte reads.  A short write on a device with an
errno of ENOSPC is a full disk: the bell, "This file system is full!.", the
bell, the output closed and unlinked, the next disk asked for, and the file begun
again from the start of its input.  Any other failure is reported with perror
and the file given up.

The end, by any way, ignores the signals, closes the sixteen descriptors - the
terminal among them, so that nothing said after is heard - unmounts, and takes
the mount point away.  Only after that does it look at whether it is a good end,
and write /etc/dtab.

WHAT DOES NOT WORK IN THE ORIGINAL, and does here

-hN and -dN.  The number is converted by a routine handed 512 as the length of
the string, and it works to the string's address plus 512, which with the
arguments at the top of memory is a pointer that has wrapped; every number comes
out 0.  A -h5 reads as -h, which consumes nothing, and then a 5, which is not an
option, which is the interactive mode.  td.c converts them properly.  The
original was tested for them with a copy in which the two constants, at 01ed and
0240, were changed from 512 to 32.

THE EXIT STATUS was inverted: 1 for a good dump and 0 for every mistake.  It is
0 and 1 here.

HOW IT WAS CHECKED

In usersim on a pseudo terminal, which it requires, against the original, on a
source of nested directories, an empty one, a hidden one, a set-id file, a
read-only file, a 600000 byte file, files at 512 and 513 bytes and files of
different ages: a full dump, a verbose one, -a, -h and -d in their combinations
(on the original with the constants changed), -i with and without a dump table
and -u to make one and to update one, an unknown source, an unknown destination,
a destination that is a file, a source that is a file, a destination that
does not exist, and the interactive mode
with F, I, a bad answer, a bad source, an empty one and a letter that is not
an option.  The destination trees are compared file for file with their modes,
sizes and checksums, and the messages are compared as typed; they are the same.
The date a dump table records is the one thing masked, since the runs are not in
the same second.

The device destination was run on two disk images, one with a file system and
one without, through the media change: a return, a name typed, a name that does
not exist, a device with no file system and each answer to the question about
making one, and a device that does not exist.  usersim mounts the image but
will not write into it - creat, link and the rest refuse a path in a mounted
file system with EPERM - so the copying itself, the disk full and the next disk,
were not run.  What was compared there is the messages, in the order the
original writes them, and what each file does when it cannot be made, which
exercises the error paths of copyfile and mkpath.  The full-disk path, and the
dump table update that comes after a device dump, were read and not run.

The directory walk comes out in the order the host gives the sim, which is the
same for both.
